IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ucpr-time`

-- Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 2005 (NSW) -- the steps a claimant meets from filing to judgment, from the seat of the
-- claimant (the plaintiff).
--
-- SEAT: the person bringing a civil claim in a NSW court. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Until
-- when can I serve my statement of claim?", "Is the defendant in default?", "What default judgment can I ask for?", "By
-- when must I file my reply?", "Must I serve a statement of particulars of my injuries?", "If I discontinue, must I pay
-- the defendant's costs?", "Can the court dismiss my proceedings for lack of progress?". The court gets no seat; what it
-- may do is encoded as what it means for the claimant.
--
-- Provisions: r 6.2 (commencement and service of originating process), r 14.3 (time for defence), r 14.4 (reply),
-- r 16.1-16.10 (default judgment), r 15.12-15.17 (particulars in personal injury and death claims), r 23.1-23.3 (medical
-- examination expenses), r 12.1, 12.3, 12.4 and 42.19 (discontinuance), r 12.8, 12.9 (dismissal for lack of progress),
-- with r 1.11 and 1.12 (time) from ucpr-time.l4.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the rule's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if" or "only", a flat negative, a period the rule fixes), or NOTHING where the rules do not
-- answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment. "Unless the court orders otherwise" is stated
-- in the question, not decided.
--
-- Three time limits are written as regulative rules (service of originating process, r 6.2(4); the defence, r 14.3; and,
-- in ucpr-offers-costs.l4, payment under an accepted offer, r 20.26(8)) and run by EVALTRACE inside exported questions
-- that take only plain facts. Time is counted in days from the day the period runs from.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 2005 -- from filing to judgment`

§§ `The nouns`

-- The parties and acts of the regulative rules in this module.
DECLARE `Proceedings party` IS ONE OF `the plaintiff`, `the defendant`
DECLARE `Proceedings act` IS ONE OF `serve the originating process`, `file a defence`

-- The answer to "is my originating process still good for service?" (r 6.2(4), (5)).
DECLARE `Service of my originating process` IS ONE OF
    `served while it was valid for service`
    `not yet served, and still valid for service`
    `no longer valid for service, but I may start fresh proceedings by filing another originating process`

-- What the claim against a defendant in default is for (r 16.4-16.8, 16.10).
DECLARE `What my claim is for` IS ONE OF
    `possession of land only`
    `detention of goods only`
    `a debt or other liquidated claim`
    `unliquidated damages only`
    `two or more of those claims and nothing else`
    `something else`

-- The default judgment the plaintiff may apply for (r 16.4-16.10).
DECLARE `Default judgment I may ask for` IS ONE OF
    `judgment for possession of the land, and costs`
    `judgment for delivery of the goods or for their value, at my option, and costs`
    `judgment for a sum not exceeding the sum claimed, interest up to judgment, and costs`
    `judgment for damages to be assessed, and costs`
    `judgment on any of those claims as if it were my only claim`
    `such judgment as I appear to be entitled to on my statement of claim`

-- The statement of particulars a personal injury or death claimant must serve (r 15.12-15.17).
DECLARE `Statement of particulars` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the rule 15.12 statement of injuries, disabilities and expenses, with the documents`
    `yes: the rule 15.13 statement for each dependant, with the documents`
    `no: Division 2 of Part 15 does not apply because a motion accompanies my claim`
    `these rules do not ask for one`

-- The answer to "can I claim the same relief again after discontinuing?" (r 12.3, 12.4).
DECLARE `Fresh proceedings after discontinuance` IS ONE OF
    `yes`
    `yes, but the court may stay them until I pay the costs of the discontinued proceedings`
    `no: the terms of the consent or leave prevent it`

-- The answer to "can the court dismiss my proceedings of its own motion for lack of progress?" (r 12.8, 12.9).
DECLARE `Dismissal for lack of progress` IS ONE OF
    `yes, after notice to me and a reasonable opportunity to be heard`
    `yes, without notice, because the proceedings are in the Possession List and no step has been taken for over 9 months`
    `yes, without notice to me`
    `no, rules 12.8 and 12.9 do not allow it on these facts`
    `rules 12.8 and 12.9 do not deal with these proceedings`

§§ `r 6.2 -- service of originating process`

-- r 6.2(3) "Originating process must be served on each defendant." r 6.2(4) "originating process is valid for service--
-- (a) in the case of proceedings in the Supreme Court, the Land and Environment Court, the Dust Diseases Tribunal or the
-- Local Court, for 6 months after the date on which it is filed, or (b) in the case of proceedings in the District Court--
-- (i) for 6 months ... (A) if it is a statement of claim seeking relief in relation only to a debt or other liquidated
-- claim, or (B) if the defendant (or at least one of the defendants) is to be served outside New South Wales, or (ii) for
-- one month after the date on which it is filed, in any other case." r 1.11(2) leaves out the filing day; r 1.11(4) moves a
-- closed last day to the next open day (fork F4); r 1.12 lets the court extend the time.
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`, `only a debt or liquidated claim` IS A BOOLEAN, `a defendant is to be served outside NSW` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the months of validity for service in` `the court` `only a debt or liquidated claim` `a defendant is to be served outside NSW` MEANS
    IF `the court` EQUALS `the District Court`
    THEN IF `only a debt or liquidated claim` OR `a defendant is to be served outside NSW`
         THEN 6
         ELSE 1
    ELSE 6

@export Until what day can I serve my statement of claim or summons on the defendant (r 6.2(4), r 1.11, r 1.12)?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceedings are in. One of: "the Supreme Court", "the District Court", "the Local Court", "the Land and Environment Court", "the Dust Diseases Tribunal"
      `my claim is a statement of claim only for a debt or other liquidated claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your originating process is a statement of claim seeking relief only for a debt or other liquidated (fixed) sum
      `a defendant is to be served outside NSW` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have to serve at least one defendant outside New South Wales
      `the day I filed it` IS A DATE @desc The day your statement of claim or summons was filed
      `the court has extended the time for service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has made an order extending the time for serving it (r 1.12)
      `the last day the court allowed` IS A DATE @desc The last day for service the court's order allows (any day if there is no order)
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the period on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
GIVETH A DATE
`Until when can I serve my originating process` MEANS
    IF `the court has extended the time for service`
    THEN `the last day the court allowed`
    ELSE `the last day of` (`the months of validity for service in` `the court` `my claim is a statement of claim only for a debt or other liquidated claim` `a defendant is to be served outside NSW`) `months after` `the day I filed it` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`)

-- The validity period as a rule: the plaintiff must serve within it. Jurally r 6.2(5) makes this a liability, not a duty
-- with a sanction: an unserved process lapses and fresh proceedings may be started. Time 0 is the filing day.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Proceedings party` `Proceedings act`
`r 6.2 -- the plaintiff must serve the originating process while it is valid for service` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the plaintiff`
    MUST `serve the originating process`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `the plaintiff` BECAUSE "originating process not served while valid for service (r 6.2(4)); fresh proceedings may be started (r 6.2(5))"

@export Was my statement of claim or summons served while it was still valid, and if not, what can I do (r 6.2(3)-(5))?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceedings are in. One of: "the Supreme Court", "the District Court", "the Local Court", "the Land and Environment Court", "the Dust Diseases Tribunal"
      `my claim is a statement of claim only for a debt or other liquidated claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your originating process is a statement of claim seeking relief only for a debt or other liquidated (fixed) sum
      `a defendant is to be served outside NSW` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have to serve at least one defendant outside New South Wales
      `the day I filed it` IS A DATE @desc The day your statement of claim or summons was filed
      `the court has extended the time for service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has made an order extending the time for serving it (r 1.12)
      `the last day the court allowed` IS A DATE @desc The last day for service the court's order allows (any day if there is no order)
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the period on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
      `I have served it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served it on the defendant
      `the day I served it` IS A DATE @desc The day you served it (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about
GIVETH A `Service of my originating process`
`Was my originating process served in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`r 6.2 -- the plaintiff must serve the originating process while it is valid for service` (`the days from` `the day I filed it` `to` `the last day`)) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `served while it was valid for service`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
        THEN `not yet served, and still valid for service`
        ELSE `no longer valid for service, but I may start fresh proceedings by filing another originating process`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `Until when can I serve my originating process` `the court` `my claim is a statement of claim only for a debt or other liquidated claim` `a defendant is to be served outside NSW` `the day I filed it` `the court has extended the time for service` `the last day the court allowed` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `I have served it`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day I filed it` `to` `the day I served it`)), (EVENT `the plaintiff` `serve the originating process` (`the days from` `the day I filed it` `to` `the day I served it`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day I filed it` `to` `today`))

§§ `r 14.3, 16.1, 16.2 -- the defence, and when the defendant is in default`

-- r 14.3(1) "the time limited for a defendant to file a defence is 28 days after service on the defendant of the statement
-- of claim or such other time as the court directs for the filing of a defence." With r 1.11(2) and (4).
@export By what day must the defendant file its defence to my statement of claim (r 14.3, r 1.11)?
GIVEN `the day I served the statement of claim` IS A DATE @desc The day the statement of claim was served on the defendant
      `the court has directed another time for the defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed a different time for the defendant to file its defence (or allowed it further time)
      `the last day the court directed` IS A DATE @desc The last day for the defence under that direction (any day if there is none)
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the period on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the defendant file a defence` MEANS
    IF `the court has directed another time for the defence`
    THEN `the last day the court directed`
    ELSE `the last day of` 28 `days after` `the day I served the statement of claim` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`)

-- The defence period as a rule. Time 0 is the day of service.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Proceedings party` `Proceedings act`
`r 14.3 -- the defendant must file a defence within the time limited` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the defendant`
    MUST `file a defence`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `the defendant` BECAUSE "no defence filed within the time limited by r 14.3(1) or allowed by the court: in default (r 16.2(1)(a))"

-- r 16.1 "This Part applies to proceedings commenced by statement of claim." A scope limit with no "only": proceedings
-- commenced by summons answer NOTHING. r 16.2(1) "A defendant is in default ... (a) if the defendant fails to file a
-- defence within the time limited by rule 14.3(1) or within such further time as the court allows, or (b) if the
-- defendant fails to file any affidavit verifying his or her defence in accordance with any requirement of these rules,
-- or (c) if, the defendant having duly filed a defence, the court orders the defence to be struck out." r 16.2(2)
-- "Despite subrule (1), a defendant is not in default if the defendant-- (a) has made a payment towards a liquidated claim
-- under rule 6.17, or (b) has filed an acknowledgment of claim under rule 20.34, or (c) has filed a defence after the time
-- limited ... but before a default judgment is given against the defendant." The question is asked before any default
-- judgment is given.
@export Is the defendant in default, so that I can apply for default judgment and carry on against any other party (r 16.1-16.3)?
GIVEN `I started by statement of claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you started the proceedings by statement of claim (not by summons)
      `the day I served the statement of claim` IS A DATE @desc The day the statement of claim was served on the defendant
      `the court has directed another time for the defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed a different time for the defendant to file its defence (or allowed it further time)
      `the last day the court directed` IS A DATE @desc The last day for the defence under that direction (any day if there is none)
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the period on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
      `the defendant has filed a defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has filed a defence (at any time up to today)
      `the day the defence was filed` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant filed its defence (any day if it has not)
      `the defendant failed to file a required affidavit verifying its defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has failed to file an affidavit verifying its defence that the rules require (r 14.23 requires one in the Supreme and District Courts, unless excused)
      `the court has struck out the defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered the defendant's defence to be struck out
      `the defendant paid my liquidated claim under rule 6.17` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has paid the amount claimed, interest and the fixed costs under r 6.17 (liquidated claims only)
      `the defendant filed an acknowledgment of my claim under rule 20.34` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has filed a statement acknowledging the whole amount of your liquidated claim (r 20.34)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the defendant in default` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I started by statement of claim`) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a scope limit with no "only": r 16.1 says the Part applies to proceedings commenced by statement of claim and is silent on others)
           IF `the defendant paid my liquidated claim under rule 6.17` OR `the defendant filed an acknowledgment of my claim under rule 20.34` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the court has struck out the defence` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF `the defendant has filed a defence` AND `the defendant failed to file a required affidavit verifying its defence` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`r 14.3 -- the defendant must file a defence within the time limited` (`the days from` `the day I served the statement of claim` `to` `the last day`)) 0 `the events`
               WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST FALSE
               OTHERWISE
                   IF `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
                   THEN JUST FALSE
                   ELSE IF `a defence was filed by today`
                        THEN JUST FALSE
                        ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `By when must the defendant file a defence` `the day I served the statement of claim` `the court has directed another time for the defence` `the last day the court directed` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`
        `a defence was filed by today` MEANS `the defendant has filed a defence` AND `the day the defence was filed` `is on or before` `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `a defence was filed by today`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day I served the statement of claim` `to` `the day the defence was filed`)), (EVENT `the defendant` `file a defence` (`the days from` `the day I served the statement of claim` `to` `the day the defence was filed`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day I served the statement of claim` `to` `today`))

-- r 16.3(4) "Unless the court orders otherwise, an affidavit in support is valid for the purposes of an application only if
-- it has been sworn within 14 days before the date on which the application is filed." An "only if": JUST FALSE outside.
-- Sworn on the filing day itself is taken as within (fork F5).
@export Is my affidavit in support of a default judgment application recent enough (r 16.3(4))?
GIVEN `the day I swore the affidavit` IS A DATE @desc The day your affidavit in support was sworn or affirmed
      `the day I file the application` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or will file) the application for default judgment
      `the court has ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered that an older affidavit may be used
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my affidavit in support recent enough` MEANS
    IF `the court has ordered otherwise`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF     (`the day I file the application` `plus days` (0 MINUS 14)) `is on or before` `the day I swore the affidavit`
            AND `the day I swore the affidavit` `is on or before` `the day I file the application`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 16.4(1) possession of land only: "judgment may be given ... for possession of land ... and for costs"; r 16.5(1)
-- detention of goods only: delivery of the goods or their value, "at the plaintiff's option", and costs; r 16.6(1) a debt
-- or liquidated claim (or unliquidated damages of the kind in r 14.13(2), the Local Court motor vehicle and property
-- damage claims): "a sum not exceeding the sum claimed ... interest up to judgment ... costs"; r 16.7(1) unliquidated
-- damages only: "damages to be assessed and ... costs"; r 16.8(1) two or more of those claims and no other: judgment "on
-- any of those claims as if it were the plaintiff's only claim"; r 16.10 whatever the claims: "such judgment ... as the
-- plaintiff appears to be entitled to on his or her statement of claim".
@export If the defendant is in default, what judgment can I apply for (r 16.4-16.8, 16.10)?
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `What my claim is for` @desc What your claim against the defendant in default is for. One of: "possession of land only", "detention of goods only", "a debt or other liquidated claim" (including a Local Court claim for motor vehicle or property damage costs that r 14.13(2) lets you state as an amount), "unliquidated damages only", "two or more of those claims and nothing else", "something else"
GIVETH A `Default judgment I may ask for`
`What default judgment can I ask for` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my claim`
    WHEN `possession of land only` THEN `judgment for possession of the land, and costs`
    WHEN `detention of goods only` THEN `judgment for delivery of the goods or for their value, at my option, and costs`
    WHEN `a debt or other liquidated claim` THEN `judgment for a sum not exceeding the sum claimed, interest up to judgment, and costs`
    WHEN `unliquidated damages only` THEN `judgment for damages to be assessed, and costs`
    WHEN `two or more of those claims and nothing else` THEN `judgment on any of those claims as if it were my only claim`
    OTHERWISE `such judgment as I appear to be entitled to on my statement of claim`

§§ `r 14.4 -- reply`

-- r 14.4 "(1) In proceedings in the Supreme Court or the District Court, a plaintiff may file a reply to a defence. (2) In
-- proceedings in the Local Court, a plaintiff may file a reply to a defence only by leave of the Court. (3) The time limited
-- for the plaintiff to file a reply is 14 days after service of the defence on the plaintiff." Other courts are outside
-- the rule's words: NOTHING.
@export May I file a reply to the defendant's defence (r 14.4(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceedings are in. One of: "the Supreme Court", "the District Court", "the Local Court", "the Land and Environment Court", "the Dust Diseases Tribunal"
      `the court has given me leave to reply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has given you leave to file a reply (needed in the Local Court)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I file a reply` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the court`
    WHEN `the Supreme Court` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `the District Court` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `the Local Court` THEN IF `the court has given me leave to reply` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: r 14.4(1), (2) name only the Supreme, District and Local Courts)

@export By what day must I file my reply to the defence (r 14.4(3), r 1.11)?
GIVEN `the day the defence was served on me` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant's defence was served on you
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the period on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I file my reply` MEANS
    `the last day of` 14 `days after` `the day the defence was served on me` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`)

§§ `r 15.12-15.17 -- particulars in personal injury and death claims`

-- r 15.12(1) "This rule applies to a claim for damages in respect of personal injuries arising from any event (the
-- accident), other than a claim that is the subject of proceedings under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897." (2) "On
-- or as soon as practicable after serving the statement of claim, the plaintiff must serve ... a statement accompanied by
-- [documents]". r 15.13 is the same for a Compensation to Relatives Act claim. r 15.17 "This Division does not apply to a
-- claim that is accompanied by-- (a) a notice of motion seeking a separate trial on the question of liability, or (b) a
-- notice of motion seeking an order to defer the application of this Division". A claim that is not for personal injury
-- or death is outside the scope (no "only"): NOTHING.
@export Must I serve a statement of particulars of my injury or dependency claim, with the supporting documents (r 15.12, 15.13, 15.17)?
GIVEN `my claim is for damages for personal injuries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for damages in respect of personal injuries arising from an event (an accident)
      `my claim is under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are a claim by relatives for a death under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897
      `a motion for a separate trial on liability accompanies my claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is accompanied by a notice of motion seeking a separate trial on the question of liability
      `a motion to defer Division 2 accompanies my claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is accompanied by a notice of motion seeking an order to defer the application of Division 2 of Part 15
GIVETH A `Statement of particulars`
`Must I serve a statement of particulars` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my claim is for damages for personal injuries`) AND (NOT `my claim is under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897`)
    THEN `these rules do not ask for one` -- NOTHING-kind (a scope limit with no "only": r 15.12 and 15.13 say which claims they apply to)
    ELSE IF `a motion for a separate trial on liability accompanies my claim` OR `a motion to defer Division 2 accompanies my claim`
         THEN `no: Division 2 of Part 15 does not apply because a motion accompanies my claim`
         ELSE IF `my claim is under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897`
              THEN `yes: the rule 15.13 statement for each dependant, with the documents`
              ELSE `yes: the rule 15.12 statement of injuries, disabilities and expenses, with the documents`

-- r 15.14(4) "Unless the court orders otherwise, the plaintiff must file a copy of the final statement of particulars at
-- least 42 days prior to the date fixed for hearing or arbitration of the proceedings." With r 1.11(2) and (4) (fork F3).
@export By what day must I file my final statement of particulars before the hearing (r 15.14(4), r 1.11)?
GIVEN `the day fixed for the hearing` IS A DATE @desc The date fixed for the hearing or arbitration of your proceedings
      `the court has ordered another day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered a different time for filing the final statement
      `the day the court ordered` IS A DATE @desc The last day under that order (any day if there is none)
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around that time on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I file my final statement of particulars` MEANS
    IF `the court has ordered another day`
    THEN `the day the court ordered`
    ELSE `the last day at least` 42 `days before` `the day fixed for the hearing` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`)

§§ `r 23.1-23.3 -- medical examination expenses`

-- r 23.1(1) the Division applies where "a person's physical or mental condition is relevant to a matter in question" and
-- that person is a party (or a person for whose benefit a party claims under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897).
-- r 23.3 "A party who serves a notice for medical examination must, on request by the first party, pay to the first party a
-- reasonable sum to meet the travelling and other expenses of the person concerned of and incidental to the medical
-- examination, including the expenses of having a medical expert chosen by the person attend the examination." "On
-- request" is read as a condition: no request, JUST FALSE (fork F6). Outside r 23.1: NOTHING.
@export If the defendant serves a notice asking me to attend a medical examination, must it pay my travelling and other expenses (r 23.1-23.3)?
GIVEN `my physical or mental condition is relevant to the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your physical or mental condition is relevant to a matter in question in the proceedings
      `I am a party, or claimed for under the Compensation to Relatives Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party, or a person for whose benefit a party claims under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897
      `the defendant served a notice for my medical examination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another party has served a notice asking that you be examined by a named medical expert
      `I asked it to pay my expenses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or the party claiming for you) have asked that party to pay your expenses
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the defendant pay my medical examination expenses` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my physical or mental condition is relevant to the case`) OR (NOT `I am a party, or claimed for under the Compensation to Relatives Act`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a scope limit with no "only": r 23.1(1) says when the Division applies)
    ELSE IF `the defendant served a notice for my medical examination` AND `I asked it to pay my expenses`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `r 12.1, 12.3, 12.4, 42.19 -- discontinuing my proceedings`

-- r 12.1(1) "The plaintiff ... may, by filing a notice of discontinuance, discontinue the proceedings ... (a) with the
-- consent of each other active party in the proceedings, or (b) with the leave of the court." A grant limited in its own
-- words: without consent or leave the rules do not answer (fork F7).
@export Can I discontinue my proceedings by filing a notice of discontinuance (r 12.1)?
GIVEN `every other active party consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each other active party in the proceedings consents to the discontinuance
      `the court gives me leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has given you leave to discontinue
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I discontinue my proceedings` MEANS
    IF `every other active party consents` OR `the court gives me leave`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited in its own wording: r 12.1 permits discontinuance with consent or leave and says nothing of other cases)

-- r 42.19(2) "Unless the court orders otherwise or the notice referred to in rule 12.1(2) otherwise provides, the plaintiff
-- must pay such of the defendant's costs as, at the date on which the notice of discontinuance was filed, had been
-- incurred by the defendant in relation to each claim in respect of which the proceedings have been discontinued."
-- (3) the defendant's costs in a District Court appeal under s 91 of the Children and Young Persons (Care and Protection)
-- Act 1998 "are not payable by the plaintiff unless the court finds there are special circumstances". Where the court or
-- the notice provides otherwise, what it provides governs: NOTHING (fork F8).
@export If I discontinue, must I pay the defendant's costs incurred up to the day I file the notice of discontinuance (r 42.19)?
GIVEN `the court has ordered otherwise about costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has made a different order about the costs of the discontinuance
      `the notice of discontinuance provides otherwise about costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice of discontinuance (or the consents filed with it) provides otherwise about costs
      `it is an appeal under section 91 of the care and protection Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are an appeal to the District Court under s 91 of the Children and Young Persons (Care and Protection) Act 1998
      `the court finds special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For such an appeal: whether the court finds special circumstances justifying an order that you pay
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I pay the defendant's costs if I discontinue` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the court has ordered otherwise about costs` OR `the notice of discontinuance provides otherwise about costs` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources: the order or the notice governs)
           IF `it is an appeal under section 91 of the care and protection Act` THEN IF `the court finds special circumstances` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- r 12.3 "(1) A discontinuance ... does not prevent the plaintiff from claiming the same relief in fresh proceedings.
-- (2) Subrule (1) is subject to the terms of any consent to the discontinuance or of any leave to discontinue." r 12.4 if
-- the plaintiff is liable for the costs of the discontinued proceedings and, before paying them, sues the other party
-- again "on the same or substantially the same cause of action", "the court may stay the further proceedings until those
-- costs are paid".
@export After I discontinue, can I claim the same relief again in fresh proceedings (r 12.3, 12.4)?
GIVEN `the consent or leave was on terms that prevent fresh proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consent to the discontinuance, or the court's leave, was given on terms that stop you suing again
      `I owe costs of the discontinued proceedings that I have not paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are liable to pay the other party's costs of the discontinued proceedings and have not yet paid them
      `the fresh proceedings are on the same or substantially the same cause of action against that party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the fresh proceedings are against that party on the same or substantially the same cause of action
GIVETH A `Fresh proceedings after discontinuance`
`Can I claim the same relief again after discontinuing` MEANS
    IF `the consent or leave was on terms that prevent fresh proceedings`
    THEN `no: the terms of the consent or leave prevent it`
    ELSE IF `I owe costs of the discontinued proceedings that I have not paid` AND `the fresh proceedings are on the same or substantially the same cause of action against that party`
         THEN `yes, but the court may stay them until I pay the costs of the discontinued proceedings`
         ELSE `yes`

§§ `r 12.8, 12.9 -- dismissal of my proceedings for lack of progress (if they have stalled)`

-- r 12.8 (Supreme Court, Land and Environment Court) "(2) The court may, of its own motion, make an order dismissing the
-- proceedings if it appears from the court's records that, for over 5 months, no party to the proceedings has taken any
-- step in the proceedings. (3) Such an order may not be made-- (a) if the proceedings ... are listed for a future date, or
-- (b) if there are any notices of motion or other applications ... yet to be determined, or (c) if a party satisfies the
-- court that such an order should not be made. (4) Before such an order is made, notice of the proposed order is to be
-- given ... (7) ... without notice ... if (a) the proceedings ... are ... entered in the Possession List, and (b) ... for
-- over 9 months, no party ... has taken any step".
-- r 12.9 (District Court, Local Court, proceedings commenced by or with a statement of claim) "(2) The court may, of its
-- own motion, make an order dismissing the proceedings if-- (a) a defence or cross-claim is not filed, or (b) an
-- application for default judgment is not filed, or (c) the proceedings are not otherwise disposed of, within 9 months
-- after the statement of claim is filed. (3) Such an order may be made without notice ... (4) ... may not be made if there
-- are any notices of motion or other applications ... yet to be determined." The three limbs of r 12.9(2) are read as
-- one condition: none of those things happened within the 9 months (fork F9; finding 3).
-- "Over 5 months" and "over 9 months" are strict: the day exactly 5 (or 9) months after the last step is not over
-- (fork F10). For r 12.9 the 9 months are a period for doing the listed things, so r 1.11(4) applies to their last day.
@export Can the court dismiss my proceedings of its own motion because they have not progressed (r 12.8, 12.9)?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceedings are in. One of: "the Supreme Court", "the District Court", "the Local Court", "the Land and Environment Court", "the Dust Diseases Tribunal"
      `the day any party last took a step` IS A DATE @desc The last day on which any party took a step in the proceedings, as the court's records show (Supreme Court and Land and Environment Court)
      `the proceedings are in the Possession List` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are entered in the Supreme Court's Possession List
      `a statement of claim has been filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings were started by, or now include, a statement of claim (District Court and Local Court)
      `the day the statement of claim was filed` IS A DATE @desc The day the statement of claim was filed (any day if there is none)
      `a defence or cross-claim was filed within 9 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a defence or cross-claim was filed within 9 months after the statement of claim was filed
      `I applied for default judgment within 9 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application for default judgment was filed within 9 months after the statement of claim was filed
      `the proceedings were otherwise disposed of within 9 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings were otherwise disposed of within 9 months after the statement of claim was filed
      `the proceedings are listed for a future date` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings, or any part of them, are listed for a future date
      `a motion or application is waiting to be decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any notice of motion or other application in the proceedings is yet to be determined
      `a party satisfies the court it should not dismiss` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Supreme Court or Land and Environment Court: whether a party satisfies the court that the proceedings should not be dismissed
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the 9 months on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about
GIVETH A `Dismissal for lack of progress`
`Can the court dismiss my proceedings for lack of progress` MEANS
    IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court` OR `the court` EQUALS `the Land and Environment Court`
    THEN BRANCH IF `the proceedings are in the Possession List` AND `no step for over 9 months` AND `r 12.8(3) does not bar it`
                THEN `yes, without notice, because the proceedings are in the Possession List and no step has been taken for over 9 months`
                IF `no step for over 5 months` AND `r 12.8(3) does not bar it`
                THEN `yes, after notice to me and a reasonable opportunity to be heard`
                OTHERWISE `no, rules 12.8 and 12.9 do not allow it on these facts`
    ELSE IF `the court` EQUALS `the District Court` OR `the court` EQUALS `the Local Court`
         THEN IF (NOT `a statement of claim has been filed`)
              THEN `rules 12.8 and 12.9 do not deal with these proceedings` -- NOTHING-kind (a scope limit with no "only": r 12.9(1) applies to proceedings with a statement of claim)
              ELSE IF     `the 9 months after filing have ended`
                      AND (NOT `a defence or cross-claim was filed within 9 months`)
                      AND (NOT `I applied for default judgment within 9 months`)
                      AND (NOT `the proceedings were otherwise disposed of within 9 months`)
                      AND (NOT `a motion or application is waiting to be decided`)
                   THEN `yes, without notice to me`
                   ELSE `no, rules 12.8 and 12.9 do not allow it on these facts`
         ELSE `rules 12.8 and 12.9 do not deal with these proceedings` -- NOTHING-kind (a scope limit with no "only": r 12.8(1) and 12.9(1) name their courts)
    WHERE
        `no step for over 5 months` MEANS `is before` (`the day any party last took a step` `plus months` 5) `today`
        `no step for over 9 months` MEANS `is before` (`the day any party last took a step` `plus months` 9) `today`
        `r 12.8(3) does not bar it` MEANS (NOT `the proceedings are listed for a future date`) AND (NOT `a motion or application is waiting to be decided`) AND (NOT `a party satisfies the court it should not dismiss`)
        `the end of the 9 months` MEANS `the last day of` 9 `months after` `the day the statement of claim was filed` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`)
        `the 9 months after filing have ended` MEANS `is before` `the end of the 9 months` `today`
